  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Sunday...\n\nAs the slow moving upper level low finally makes its exit to our\neast, we will see clearing skies and temps return to average. \nWhile the next system to impact the region will zip through \nTuesday and Wednesday, the next large weather maker could impact \nus this weekend, however a lot of uncertainty remains.\n\nWhile some scattered showers will linger across fae SE Montana \nand Northern Wyoming through the night, these will be light with \nminimal accumulations. As the showers and clouds exit eastwards \nand thin out through the morning, fog and low stratus will take \ntheir place. The overall extent and density of this fog remains a\nquestion as a little wind shift could mean the difference between\nslight visibility reductions and dense fog. The short range \nmodels are really hitting the locations that saw the most\nrainfall over the weekend with Harlowton, Billings, and Hardin in\nthe bullseye of the latest HRRR runs. Previously the short range\nmodels brought dense fog all the way up to the Beartooth/Absaroka\nfoothills but have since backed off. This seems to be due to a \nsurface high over YNP that has produced some weak downslope winds \nthat are fighting off the easterlies. That being said, the entire \nforecast area has the chance of seeing fog and low clouds through \ndaybreak today. Use extra caution on your commute as visibility \ncan change rapidly over short distances. \n\nIn the wake of our weekend low, the ridge that was dominating the\nsouthern plains builds northwestwards towards our area. This will\nallow for temperatures to quickly rebound to near average today \nand then push above average for the rest of the work week. \nReadings in the 80s are possible for many locations Tuesday \nthrough Friday so do not put away the t-shirts just yet, even with\nTuesday being the start of astronomical autumn. \n\nLate Tuesday into Wednesday, remnant energy will ride along the \njet out of the Desert SW through our area. This will provide a \nchance for some quick hitting showers and thunderstorms. There is \na potential that a storm or two in far eastern parts of the CWA \ncould produce some gusty winds or hail but we are simply still too \nfar out to get a good look at the event. Do note though that any \nsevere weather will be very hard to come by with this setup. \n\nThis upcoming weekend is our next shot at a potentially impactful\nweather system. As of right now there is still a lot of spread\namongst models and ensemble members too. Right now the mean\nsolutions take the low just to our north which would result in \nminimal precip and a potential strong wind event Saturday. Do not \nalter any plans yet though as this forecast will almost certainly \nchange as we get closer to the event and start to see more \nagreement amongst models. WMR",
